<p>The accused moved the High Court in criminal revision. His case was simple: the charge was framed in violation of natural justice and of the fair-trial guarantee, because the foundation of any discharge plea — the complete prosecution record — had been withheld despite the court&rsquo;s own order.</p>

<h2>The statutory architecture</h2>
<p>The prosecution leaned on Section 35(1) POCSO — the child&rsquo;s evidence must be recorded within thirty days of cognizance — read with Section 31, which applies the general criminal procedure to POCSO proceedings &ldquo;save as otherwise provided&rdquo;. The argument was that the special statute&rsquo;s urgency compresses the accused&rsquo;s procedural room. Dr. Justice Mukherjee held the opposite: POCSO contains no charge or discharge procedure of its own, so the BNSS applies in full. The court put it in terms of first principles: &ldquo;the time frame as prescribed under BNSS, which is a subsequent general statute would apply proprio vigore&rdquo; (para 24). And the obligation to supply the papers is not the accused&rsquo;s burden to chase: &ldquo;supply of prosecution papers is an obligation of the court&rdquo; (para 9). The charge, the court found, had been framed &ldquo;without supplying complete set of copies of documents as directed by the court itself on 24.10.2025&rdquo; (para 35).</p>
<p>The decision then fixes the point of real practical value: for cases before Special Courts, the sixty-day window within which a discharge application may be made runs from the date the complete set of prosecution documents is supplied — not from committal or first appearance in the abstract. An accused kept waiting for the record cannot be timed out of the discharge remedy he was never equipped to exercise.</p>

<h2>Old code, new code</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>CrPC regime.</strong> Supply of documents under Sections 207/208; discharge before the Sessions Court under Section 227; charge under Section 228. The discharge stage presupposed a complete record.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>BNSS regime.</strong> Supply of documents under Sections 230/231; discharge under Section 250 — with an express time window — and charge under Section 251. The window is now statutory, which makes the starting point decisive; this decision anchors it to the supply of the full papers.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why it matters beyond one case</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Special-statute speed does not cancel general-code safeguards. Trial timelines under POCSO discipline the court and the prosecution; they are not a licence to skip the accused&rsquo;s stages.</p>
  <p>Defence practitioners should put document deficiencies on the record immediately and in writing — the application of 24 September 2025 is what made the later charge indefensible.</p>
  <p>For prosecutors, the lesson is administrative: complete the Section 230 BNSS supply before pressing for charge, or risk losing months to a revision that was entirely avoidable.</p>
  <p>The remedial template is also worth noting: set aside the charge, two weeks to supply documents, sixty days to apply for discharge from receipt, sixty days to decide — a complete, time-bound reset rather than an open-ended remand.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This is an informational note on a Calcutta High Court decision. The BNSS provisions it applies are uniform across India, and the reasoning will be of direct relevance in POCSO trials before Special Courts in Delhi as well.</div>
